  "answer_text": "Hipparchus of Nicaea (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, geographer, and mathematician. Often called the \"Father of Astronomy,\" he is credited with several groundbreaking inventions, mathematical tools, and observational systems that revolutionized how humans map both the Earth and the heavens.\n\nHis most notable inventions and scientific creations include:\n\n### 1. Trigonometry and the Table of Chords\nHipparchus is widely considered the **founder of trigonometry**. To solve complex astronomical problems, he invented the first known **table of chords**. A \"chord\" is a straight line segment connecting two points on a curve, and his table was the historical predecessor to modern sine tables. This allowed astronomers to solve any triangle (spherical trigonometry) without having to calculate every equation from scratch.\n\n### 2. The Planispheric Astrolabe\nHipparchus is credited as the likely inventor of the **planispheric astrolabe**. This handheld device allowed astronomers, navigators, and astrologers to determine the time of day or night, track the positions of the sun and stars, and calculate latitude. He achieved this by utilizing **stereographic projection**\u2014a mathematical method he developed to project a three-dimensional celestial sphere onto a flat, two-dimensional plane.\n\n### 3. The Stellar Magnitude Scale\nTo categorize the stars he observed, Hipparchus invented the **stellar magnitude scale** to measure star brightness. He divided the stars into six classes:\n* **First magnitude:** The brightest stars in the night sky.\n* **Sixth magnitude:** The faintest stars barely visible to the naked human eye.\nModern astronomers still use a highly refined, mathematical version of this exact scale today.\n\n### 4. The First Comprehensive Star Catalog\nUsing his instruments, Hipparchus mapped the night sky and created the **first systematic star catalog** in the Western world. It contained the precise coordinates (using a two-coordinate celestial grid) and brightness classifications of approximately 850 stars. This monument of ancient science allowed future astronomers to track changes in the night sky over centuries.\n\n### 5. The Hipparchan Dioptra (Astronomical Sighting Tool)\nHipparchus invented (or heavily improved upon) the **dioptra**, an optical sighting instrument. His design featured a long, grooved rod with a fixed sighting pinhole at one end and a sliding plate at the other. By sliding the plate until it perfectly blocked out the sun or moon, he could measure their apparent angular diameters with remarkable precision. \n\n### 6. Terrestrial Latitude and Longitude\nWhile the basic idea of a grid was proposed before him, Hipparchus was the first to systematically apply rigorous mathematical principles to geography by **defining locations on Earth using latitude and longitude**. He divided the Earth's grid into 360 degrees. He also proposed a brilliant, theoretically sound method to find longitude: comparing the local time of a lunar eclipse from different parts of the world to measure the distance between them.\n\n### 7. The Armillary Sphere (Reputed)\nThough its exact origins are debated, many historical sources credit Hipparchus with inventing or significantly improving the **armillary sphere**\u2014a physical model of objects in the sky consisting of a spherical framework of rings, centered on Earth, that represented lines of celestial longitude and latitude.\n\n---\n\n### His Greatest *Discovery*: Precession of the Equinoxes\nWhile not a physical invention, Hipparchus's tools and star catalog led to his most famous scientific discovery: the **precession of the equinoxes**. By comparing his own precise observations with those of astronomers from centuries prior, he realized that the positions of the stars were slowly shifting over time. He correctly deduced that the Earth's rotational axis gradually \"wobbles\" like a spinning top, a cycle that takes roughly 26,000 years to complete.",
